window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][133] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A large online marketplace is considering selling its own branded products in categories where third-party sellers do well. Should it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, own brands have higher margins,&quot; or &quot;no, it will upset sellers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs margin against seller trust and regulatory risk, with numbers.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Models the net including diverted commission and trust effects, identifies the mechanism of the marketplace advantage (selection, trust), proposes safeguards, and states a test and conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I know the topic only from public information, and nothing here describes any real company's practice. Marketplaces and their owners' own products have been the subject of public scrutiny and regulatory attention in several jurisdictions <code>[V: European Commission decision of 20 December 2022 on Amazon's use of seller data and Buy Box; Digital Markets Act self-preferencing rule]</code>, which is part of the risk. <em>Diagnosis.</em> A marketplace's advantage is selection and trust: buyers come because it has what they want and they can rely on the process; sellers come because buyers are there (a network effect, C18.5). The question is whether competing with sellers erodes that advantage. <em>Numbers (illustrative).</em> A category has gross merchandise value of $1 billion. The marketplace earns a 15% commission: $150 million. Suppose its own brand captures 10% of the category ($100 million of sales) at a 35% margin: $35 million. But those sales come largely from sellers' sales, so the commission on them is lost: 15% × $100 million = $15 million. Net: +$20 million. Now suppose sellers respond: a few leave and others raise prices, and the category's total sales fall 3% ($30 million), costing $4.5 million of commission. Net: +$15.5 million. If the response is worse (a 10% fall, $100 million, $15 million of commission), the net is +$5 million and the long-run damage could exceed it. The estimate of the seller and buyer response is the swing assumption. <em>Beyond the arithmetic.</em> Trust has a multiplier: sellers make decisions about where to invest based on whether the platform will use their success against them. Concerns include use of sellers' sales data to choose what to copy, favored placement for own products, and the effect on buyer trust if results look biased. Regulation can change the economics overnight. <em>Options.</em> (A) No own brands. (B) Own brands only where sellers are thin (gaps in selection), which fills selection and competes less directly. (C) Own brands with strict safeguards: no use of individual sellers' data, clear labeling, no preferential ranking, and independent audit. (D) Free entry in all categories. <em>Recommendation.</em> B with C's safeguards: enter where selection or quality is poor, which expands the marketplace's value, and keep firm rules on data and ranking. Measure: seller retention and investment, buyer trust, category growth, and the diverted-commission arithmetic, with a stop rule if seller exits exceed a threshold. What would change my mind: evidence that sellers in the category are leaving or that regulators are acting, which would favor A. <em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only (a large marketplace operator): <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that marketplace-conflict prompts appear at Amazon <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about Amazon's actual practices, data or decisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your productivity-software company wants to add voice transcription to its notes product. Should it build the capability, buy a company that has it, or partner with a provider?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build it, because we should own our technology,&quot; or &quot;partner, because it's faster.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares the options on cost, time and control, and says whether the capability is core.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Decides by whether the capability differentiates the product, models the volume at which build overtakes partner, prices the delay as well as the spend, protects against dependence (data, terms, exit), and says what would change the choice.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Start with the question that decides the rest (the chooser is FW-C18-07).</em> Is transcription something customers choose us for, or something they expect as a basic part of notes? If it is a basic expectation, speed and cost matter most and ownership matters least; if it is where we want to win (for example, a unique accuracy on meetings), control and learning matter more. <em>Options and figures (all invented).</em> Partner: license a provider at $0.012 a minute with about six weeks to integrate. Build: about $3.0 million and twelve months, with a run cost of $0.004 a minute. Buy: a company at about $25 million with a team of twenty engineers and a small user base. At 10 million minutes a month, partner costs 0.012 × 10,000,000 × 12 = $1.44 million a year and build costs $0.48 million a year to run, so build's extra $3.0 million is repaid in 3.0 ÷ (1.44 − 0.48) = about 3.1 years. At 30 million minutes a month, partner costs $4.32 million a year against $1.44 million, and build is repaid in about one year. So volume decides when building pays. <em>Delay has a price.</em> Building takes twelve months against six weeks: if the feature is expected to lift retention or win deals, those twelve months of lost benefit belong in the comparison, as does the risk that a competitor ships first. <em>Risks of each.</em> Partner: dependence on a provider's price and quality, and where our customers' audio goes (privacy and data terms). Build: delivery risk and distraction from the roadmap. Buy: integration risk and paying for a team and product we may not need; it makes sense only when the capability and the team are both scarce and strategic. <em>Recommendation.</em> Partner now, to ship in weeks and learn how customers use it, with contract terms that protect data, cap price increases and allow an exit; revisit building when volume passes about 25 million minutes a month or when accuracy for our use case becomes a reason customers choose us. Buy only if a partner cannot meet the quality bar and a team with the capability is available at a price that is small beside the value. I'd measure adoption, accuracy on our own audio, cost per minute and customer privacy concerns, and I'd change my view if the provider's terms worsened or our volume grew faster than expected. <em>Signals.</em> The core-or-context question; a computed break-even on volume; the price of delay; dependence risks; a staged recommendation with triggers.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What should the product vision be for a mid-sized photo-sharing app over the next three years, and how would you turn it into a roadmap?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A list of features: &quot;AI editing, video, a marketplace.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States who the product is for, the change it makes in their lives, and a few strategic bets, ordered in time.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Grounds the vision in user and market trends and the company's advantages, states what it will not do, converts it into outcomes and bets by horizon with metrics and kill criteria, and says how it would learn that the vision is wrong.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Ground it.</em> A vision should answer who we serve, what changes for them, and why we can win. I'd start from evidence: how people share photos now (messaging apps and private groups, more than public feeds), what our users do that is distinctive (suppose families sharing within small circles), and what we do better than large platforms (privacy, a calm experience, long-term storage and organization). I'd also name the trends that could hurt us: sharing moving to messaging, and phone makers building photo features into the device. <em>A vision statement.</em> &quot;The place where a family's photos live and are shared with the people who matter, for decades.&quot; It is specific about the user (families and close circles), the job (keep and share), and the quality (private and lasting). It leaves out public fame-seeking and ad-driven feeds, which is a strategic choice: what we will not do. <em>From vision to outcomes by horizon.</em> Now (next two quarters): make sharing within circles effortless, measured by shares per active family and the share of new users who invite a second person within a week (say from 28% to 40%). Next (quarters three to six): make the archive worth keeping, through search by person and event and annual books, measured by weekly retrievals per user and 12-month retention. Later (year two and three): extend to shared family history and relatives who are not users, measured by non-user recipients who become users and by paid conversion. For each bet I'd write the assumption, a small test and a kill criterion (C26), so that the roadmap is a set of testable bets, not promised features. <em>Guardrails and honesty.</em> The roadmap should commit to outcomes and ranges, and show confidence falling with distance: high for now, medium for next, low for later. I'd keep capacity for the unplanned and review the vision yearly against what the data says. <em>How I'd know it's wrong.</em> Leading indicators: if circles do not grow, if retrieval stays low, or if shares move to messaging apps faster than we can integrate. I'd set a review date and thresholds in advance, and be ready to change the target user, not just the features. <em>Signals.</em> Evidence-based user and trend grounding; a specific vision with exclusions; outcomes by horizon with metrics; bets with kill criteria; how it could be wrong.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a platform? How is a two-sided market different from a developer platform?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A platform is a big app that lots of people use.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a platform by multiple participant groups and gives both types with examples.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names the different core jobs and hard problems of each, notes hybrids, and says when the label does not apply.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A platform is a product that others build on or transact through, which creates value for more than one group. The defining feature is that the value to one group depends on another group's participation. A <em>two-sided market</em> has two groups who need each other: riders and drivers, buyers and sellers. The platform's core job is matching and trust. The hard problems are the cold start (neither side comes until the other is there), keeping enough supply near the demand (liquidity), and disintermediation (participants bypassing the platform after a match). The primary metric is successful transactions. A <em>developer platform</em> provides a foundation that others build products on, through APIs and tools: an operating system, a cloud service, a payments API. The core job is to be reliable, well-documented and stable. The hard problems are developer experience (how fast a developer succeeds), stability (not breaking what people built), and governance. The primary metric is developers who ship to production, or what they build. Many platforms are both: an app store has developers (who build) and users (who buy), so it faces review-policy, fee and discovery problems on top of the two kinds of problem. When it does not apply: a product with many users but no interaction among them and no outside builders is just a popular product. Calling it a platform imports the wrong questions. My first step with a platform question is to name the sides and what each gives and gets.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A freelance marketplace has 5,000 freelancers and 300 clients. Clients complain they cannot find the right person, and freelancers complain they get no work. What's going on, and what do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Get more clients with marketing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Diagnoses a supply-demand imbalance and a matching problem, and proposes narrowing.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the ratio, uses liquidity, separates the two complaints, and proposes seeding demand and curating supply, with a metric.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The two complaints are one problem seen from each side. There are about 17 freelancers for each client (5,000 ÷ 300), so work is scarce for freelancers, and with so many to choose among and no structure, clients struggle to find the right person. The platform has too much unfocused supply and too little demand. <em>Diagnose by segment.</em> Not all freelancers compete for the same work. Suppose most are in a few popular categories while demand is concentrated in a few others (invented). Look at the match rate and time to hire by category: some categories may be healthy, others empty on one side. <em>What I'd do.</em> (1) <em>Narrow:</em> focus on the categories where demand exists and where the platform can reach density, and stop recruiting supply in saturated ones. (2) <em>Raise the quality of matching for clients:</em> better search, a short brief that routes to a small set of qualified candidates, so clients see a few strong options and not a thousand. (3) <em>Grow demand where supply is strong:</em> outbound to clients who need those skills. (4) <em>Curate supply:</em> verified skills and reviews, so clients can trust what they see and the best freelancers get the work, which keeps them. <em>Metric.</em> Not sign-ups. Primary: the share of client briefs that result in a hire within a week (hire rate), with time to first qualified proposal, and freelancer earnings for the top and median freelancer as guardrails (if only a handful earn, supply will leave). I'd check that clients rehire. <em>Risk.</em> Cutting supply feels like shrinking; the aim is balance. What would change my mind: if clients' real issue is trust and not selection, in which case verification and protection matter more than matching.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A payments API team can ship a cleaner design that simplifies integration for new developers but breaks 3% of existing integrations. Do you ship it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, simpler is better,&quot; or &quot;No, never break developers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs benefits for new developers against costs to existing ones, and considers versioning.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies both, proposes a non-breaking alternative (a new version alongside the old), a deprecation timeline and migration support, and treats trust as an asset.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general: I know such products only from public information, and nothing here describes any real plan. I'd not ship it as a breaking change on a flag day. I'd look for a way to get the benefit without the break. <em>Numbers (invented).</em> 200,000 businesses have integrated; 3% is 6,000. Helping each migrate costs about $150 of support and engineering time: $900,000 once. The benefit: a simpler API raises the share of new developers who reach production from 56% to 62% (a better first mile, C19.3); with 20,000 new developers a year, that is 1,200 more integrations, each worth $2,000 a year in revenue (invented): $2.4 million a year. The risk: if 5% of the 6,000 affected businesses leave because of the disruption, 300 integrations are lost at $2,000: $600,000 a year. Net in year one: $2.4 million − $0.9 million − $0.6 million = +$0.9 million. That looks positive, but it ignores trust: a platform known for breaking integrations raises the cost of building on it for everyone, and that is hard to measure and costly to lose. <em>Alternative.</em> Ship the cleaner design as a new version, and keep the old working. New developers get the simple path; existing integrations are untouched. Cost: maintaining two versions for a while. Then deprecate the old version on a published schedule (say 24 months), with notice, a migration guide and tooling, and focus support on the businesses most affected. This captures most of the $2.4 million without the $0.6 million risk and the trust cost. <em>Decision.</em> Introduce the new version, keep the old, announce a long deprecation path, and measure adoption of the new version by new and existing developers, support tickets, and churn among those who migrate. What would change my mind: if the old design carries a security flaw, a shorter timeline with strong support is justified. <em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of developer-platform prompts and <code>Public</code> that Stripe documents API versioning <code>[V: Stripe API versioning documentation; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No Stripe data or plan is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A software vendor accounts for 12% of your enterprise marketplace's revenue. It asks for a lower revenue share and a say in your roadmap. How do you respond?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Give it what it wants, it's too big to lose,&quot; or &quot;treat everyone the same.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs the vendor's value and leverage against fairness to other vendors, and offers structured options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the cost, separates the economic request from the influence request, proposes volume tiers open to all and a partner council, and addresses concentration risk and the precedent.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general: I know such platforms only from public information, and nothing here describes any real plan. Two requests are bundled and need separating: price and influence. <em>Price.</em> Suppose the marketplace's revenue is $50 million at a 20% share, so the vendor generates $6 million in revenue, on $30 million of sales. A cut to 12% would reduce revenue from that vendor to $3.6 million, a loss of $2.4 million a year. If other vendors learn of a special deal, they will ask for it, and trust in the rules erodes. If the vendor really generates 12% of revenue, it is also a concentration risk: its departure would hurt, which is a reason to reduce dependence as well as to keep it. <em>Principled answer.</em> Offer a published volume tier that any vendor can qualify for, with a lower rate above a sales threshold, perhaps with a requirement that gives the platform something in return (co-selling, deeper integration, support commitments). The vendor gets a better rate because of its size, and every other vendor can see the rule and aim for it. <em>Influence.</em> A seat on a partner advisory council, where several vendors give input to the roadmap, is reasonable; a private veto is not, because it would trade the platform's neutrality and its other vendors' trust for one account. I'd listen to the vendor's needs (the real product problems) and consider them on the same criteria as other requests. <em>Concentration.</em> Work on the broader base: grow the number of mid-sized vendors and measure the share of revenue from the top 10 (target a decline over time). <em>Decision.</em> Offer the volume tier and the council, hold on a bespoke deal, and give the vendor a dated plan for roadmap items that serve many vendors. What would change my mind: if the vendor is truly strategic (it brings customers that others do not), a bigger commitment from them in exchange for a deeper partnership, structured as a tier open to peers. <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports of enterprise partner and marketplace prompts at Microsoft <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or program is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design the review policy for an app store: speed versus safety versus developer trust.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Review everything carefully,&quot; or &quot;make it fast.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the three goals and designs a tiered process.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sorts submissions by risk, quantifies reviewer capacity, builds explanations and appeals, measures false rejections and harmful approvals, and sets governance for changes to the rules.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the topic only from public information; this describes a design for a store of the kind Apple and Google operate, not any real process. The three goals pull against each other (FW-C19-08). Strict review raises safety and costs speed and trust; light review raises speed and risk. The way through is to match scrutiny to risk. <em>Tier by risk.</em> Suppose 100,000 submissions a week (invented). Low risk (about 70%): updates to established apps with no new permissions or content types. Medium (25%): new apps in ordinary categories and updates that add features. High (5%): new apps in sensitive areas (payments, children, health, financial products), apps requesting powerful permissions, and apps from new accounts with signals of abuse. <em>Process.</em> Automated checks on everything (malware, policy violations, privacy declarations): minutes. Low risk: automated approval within an hour. Medium: automated checks plus a short human review, within 24 hours. High: deeper human review, two to three days, with specialists. <em>Capacity check.</em> Human time: 25,000 medium submissions at 0.5 hours is 12,500 hours; 5,000 high at 3 hours is 15,000 hours; total 27,500 hours a week, about 786 reviewers at 35 productive hours each. That is the real cost, and why automation and good tiering matter: each point of submissions moved from medium to low saves hundreds of reviewer hours. <em>Developer trust.</em> Published, specific rules with examples; consistent decisions (audit a sample for consistency); a reason with every rejection, tied to a rule; a fast appeals path with a different reviewer; a way to ask for guidance before submitting; predictable timing. Trust is lost mainly through opaque, inconsistent or slow decisions. <em>Safety.</em> Post-release monitoring (reports, anomaly detection), fast takedown and a way to revoke developer access; stronger identity checks for developers who handle sensitive data. <em>Measures.</em> Median and 95th-percentile time to decision by tier; harmful-approval rate (apps approved that later violate rules); false-rejection rate (rejections overturned on appeal), target under 1% of decisions; developer satisfaction; and user-reported harm. Guard against gaming: a faster median hides a long tail. <em>Governance of the rules.</em> Changes announced with notice, a comment period for significant changes, and a review of rules against outcomes each quarter. <em>Decision.</em> Tiered review with automation for the bulk, human depth where risk is high, explanations and appeals for trust, and the measures above. What would change my mind: a spike in harmful approvals (tighten the low tier) or a rise in overturned rejections (retrain reviewers or simplify rules). Regulatory requirements on app review differ by place and are changing <code>[V: Apple Form 10-Q, 2026; EU Digital Markets Act]</code>. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple publishes review guidelines and <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that platform prompts at Apple include review policy <code>[V: Apple App Review Guidelines on Apple's developer site; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported and unverified]</code>. No claim is made about any real review process, staffing or metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A home-services marketplace suspects that customers and providers are booking repeat jobs off the platform. How do you detect and reduce this?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ban contact information sharing&quot; or &quot;cut the fee.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines the problem, finds proxies to measure it, and offers value-based solutions.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the leakage, shows that a fee cut alone may not pay, designs continuing value, considers fairness to providers, and sets a test and guardrails.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Disintermediation is likely where the platform's value is the introduction and repeat jobs are easy to arrange directly. I'd start by measuring how big it is. <em>Detection.</em> Direct measurement is impossible, so use proxies: pairs of customer and provider with a first job and then no further bookings though the customer keeps using the category (the customer's other bookings continue with someone else, or provider availability shows busy); attempts to share contact details in messages; repeat-booking rates lower than in comparable categories; providers whose reviews mention working &quot;outside the app.&quot; Estimate leakage: suppose (invented) 100,000 first jobs a month, 40% of pairs would rebook, and 30% of those rebookings leak. With a 15% take rate on a $60 job ($9), leakage costs 100,000 × 0.4 × 0.3 × $9 = $108,000 a month. <em>Does cutting the fee on repeat jobs fix it?</em> Suppose repeat jobs are 40,000 a month; today 70% stay on-platform: 28,000 at $9 = $252,000. If a lower rate of 8% ($4.80) cut leakage from 30% to 10%, on-platform would be 36,000 at $4.80 = $172,800. Revenue falls. To keep revenue at $252,000 at $4.80 would need 52,500 repeat jobs on the platform, more than the 40,000 that exist. So a fee cut alone cannot pay; it must be paired with something else. <em>What works: continuing value.</em> Things the customer and provider cannot easily replicate off-platform: payment protection and dispute resolution, insurance or guarantees on damage, scheduling and reminders, recurring bookings with automatic payment, a verified record of work and reviews that the provider builds and takes pride in, financing or faster payouts for providers. If a guarantee costs $1 per job and cuts leakage from 30% to 20%, on-platform repeat jobs become 32,000 at ($9 − $1) = $256,000, which slightly exceeds today's $252,000 and builds trust. <em>Rules.</em> Contact-sharing bans have limited effect and annoy honest users; use them lightly for the first booking only, and focus on incentives. <em>Test and guardrails.</em> Pilot the guarantee and recurring-booking tools in two markets with a holdout; measure repeat rate on-platform, provider earnings, customer satisfaction and the proxy for leakage. What would change my mind: leakage proving far smaller than the proxies suggest, in which case the effort belongs elsewhere.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c19-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C19-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C19 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Define the metrics you would use to run a developer platform for a cloud API, including how you would tell early that something is going wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Number of developers and API calls.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a primary metric and metrics for acquisition, activation, usage, quality and revenue.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses time to first call and production share, adds ecosystem health (concentration, churn), builds leading indicators and guardrails, and reads trends by cohort.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start from the platform's core value: developers building products that work and keep working. A volume metric such as API calls can rise while developer satisfaction and ecosystem health fall, so I'd use a primary metric and guardrails. <em>Primary metric.</em> Developers whose integrations are live in production and active over the last 30 days (production-active developers). It reflects success, not interest. <em>The funnel (FW-C19-04).</em> Sign-ups; the share who get credentials; the share who make a first successful call, with the median time to it; the share who reach production. Each stage is a place to look for friction; the first call time is the leading indicator of adoption. <em>Usage and value.</em> API calls per production-active developer; the number of distinct endpoints used (breadth); revenue per developer, and the share of developers growing their usage. <em>Quality and trust.</em> API availability and latency at the 95th and 99th percentiles; error rate for correct requests; support tickets per 1,000 developers and time to resolve; documentation feedback; the number of breaking-change incidents. <em>Ecosystem health.</em> Concentration: the share of API volume or revenue from the top 10 developers; developer churn (production-active developers lost per month) by cohort; multi-homing (developers also using rivals) from surveys; the share of new developers coming from referrals. <em>Early warnings.</em> (1) Time to first call lengthening for new cohorts, before sign-ups fall. (2) The production share of the latest sign-up cohort falling relative to earlier cohorts. (3) Rising error rates on a version before its deprecation. (4) Support ticket growth faster than developer growth. (5) Rising concentration, meaning growth is coming from a few large accounts. Read them by cohort, since averages blend old developers who are fine with new ones who are struggling (C13, C05). <em>Guardrails.</em> No improvement in sign-ups counts if the production share falls; no change counts that raises the error rate. <em>How I'd act.</em> A weekly dashboard by side and cohort, a threshold on each early warning with an owner, and a monthly review of developer interviews alongside the numbers. What would change the set: a platform with a very different developer type (enterprise integrators versus hobbyists) would need separate funnels.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a go-to-market plan, and what is a PM's role in it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's the plan for launching a product with marketing.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the elements: customer, value, positioning, price, channels, launch, metrics.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes motions, assigns ownership across functions, ties the plan to a success measure, and gives an example.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;A go-to-market plan is how a product reaches its target customers and wins them: who they are and what problem they have, what we say to them, what it costs and how it is packaged, how they find and buy it, how we launch, and how we'll know it's working. The motion varies: product-led for a consumer app or developer tool, sales-led for enterprise, partner-led where a platform brings the customers. &quot;The PM owns the customer problem, the value and the product, and works closely with product marketing on positioning and launch, with sales and support on enablement, and with finance on price. The PM usually co-owns the positioning and pricing decisions and carries the launch. For example, when we launched an expense-capture feature for small-business owners, I defined the target (owners tracking expenses in spreadsheets), the message (receipts captured automatically), the in-product launch and the success measure: the share of active owners who capture ten receipts in two weeks, with support contacts as a guardrail.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The elements; motions; ownership; a success measure.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Write a positioning statement for a new note-taking app for graduate students.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The best note-taking app for students.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the target, need, category, benefit and alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Targets by need, states a differentiator a competitor could not claim, supports it with proof, and plans a test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;For graduate students who read hundreds of papers and lose track of where an idea came from, [the app] is a notebook that links every note to its source passage. Unlike general note apps, where you copy and paste and lose the origin, it keeps the citation attached, so writing a literature review means assembling notes you can trace. &quot;What makes it sharp: the target is defined by a need (managing sources), not by 'students'; the benefit is one thing (traceable notes); the differentiator is specific; and a competitor could not say it unless they built it. Proof: a test with ten graduate students writing a short review, measuring time to assemble and citation errors against their current tools. For messaging I'd show three headlines to twenty students and ask which they'd repeat to a labmate and what they think the app does; I'd choose the one they can repeat and understand correctly.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Need-based target; one benefit; a differentiator; proof and a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Price a new premium wearable together with its services subscription.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd price it like competitors, maybe a bit higher because it's premium.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses cost floor, willingness to pay and the subscription's attach and margin.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the floor, finds the margin-maximizing price from willingness-to-pay data, models services economics over several years, tests a trial, and states the assumption that decides it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I reason from the structure of device-plus-services businesses and claim nothing about any real product or price. &quot;I'd set the price between a cost floor and the value ceiling and treat the services as a second profit stream. The unit cost is about $200, so a 55% margin gives a floor near $445. Competitors sit between $300 and $500, and the value is a daily health insight. From a willingness-to-pay survey, the margin per respondent is highest at $449 (about 102 against 92 at $349 and 77 at $549), so I'd start at $449 and test $399 and $499, since survey intent overstates purchase. &quot;For the subscription at $9.99 a month with a 70% margin, 35% attach and 80% annual renewal, services contribute about $72 per device over three years, which is about 29% on top of the $249 device margin. A three-month free trial that lifts attach to 50% costs about $7 per device to serve and wins by about $13 over three years, but only if attach reaches about 43%; below that it loses. So I'd test the trial on half of buyers and decide on 90-day attach and retention. &quot;Positioning: premium on craft and ecosystem value, not price alone; guardrails: returns, services churn and support load.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Floor, WTP-based price, services economics, the deciding assumption, a test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you decide whether to add a new benefit to a paid membership program?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add it if customers want it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares cost per member with effects on retention and spend.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes the break-even retention gain, accounts for selection and cannibalization, designs a holdout test with guardrails, and considers honest enrollment and cancellation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I describe a method and no real program's economics <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;I'd compare the benefit's cost per member with its effect on the member's value. Suppose a member pays $120 a year and contributes $150 a year of margin on their spending, so a retained member-year is worth $270, and renewal today is 85%. The benefit costs $18 per member a year. If it lifts renewal by 1.5 points, that is worth 0.015 × $270 = $4.05; if it also raises spending by 2%, that adds $3.00; the total is $7.05 against a cost of $18, a net loss of about $11 per member. To break even the benefit would need to lift renewal by about 5.6 points ((18 − 3) ÷ 270), which is unlikely for one benefit. &quot;So I'd not add it as designed, and I'd ask what could change the math: a cheaper way to deliver it, or a benefit that targets a segment where the effect is bigger (members at risk of leaving). I'd test with a holdout, measure renewal and spending at 90 and 365 days, and watch for selection (heavy users take up the benefit most). I'd also check that the program remains honest: clear enrollment and easy cancellation, since regulators and customers care (C10.10).&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Break-even computed; selection; a test; honesty about enrollment.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy-gtm · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C20 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Should an AI assistant be sold as an add-on to a productivity suite or bundled into the suite price?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Bundle it so everyone uses it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares revenue, cost and adoption for each.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes contribution under each option, includes churn and cost to serve, considers strategic effects (adoption, data, competition), and plans a test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; nothing here describes any real product or price <code>Inferred</code>. &quot;I'd compute the contribution of each option for 1,000 users. The suite is $12 per user per month; the assistant costs about $6 per user per month to serve for a full user and $2.40 on average if the average user is lighter. <em>Add-on:</em> priced at $20, 15% attach: revenue 1,000 × $12 + 150 × $20 = $15,000; cost 150 × $6 = $900; contribution $14,100. <em>Bundle:</em> the suite price rises to $17 for everyone; suppose 5% of users leave over the increase: 950 users at $17 is $16,150; cost 950 × $2.40 = $2,280; contribution $13,870. On these numbers the add-on is slightly better; if the price rise lost only 2% of users, the bundle would be about $14,300 and ahead. &quot;So the numbers are close, and the deciding factors are strategic: a bundle puts the assistant in front of everyone, which grows usage, feedback and habit, and may defend against competitors; an add-on protects margin and lets heavy users self-select, and reveals willingness to pay. I'd propose an add-on first, with a trial for all, then move to a bundle at a higher suite price if attach and usage show that most users value it. I'd test price and packaging on a sample of enterprise customers, with guardrails on renewal and complaints.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Computed comparison; sensitivity; strategic factors; a staged plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c20-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C20-06</h3>\n";
